WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.710 --> 00:00:04.880
There aren't many superhero roles that match
up to Spider-Man in terms of cultural impact.

00:00:04.880 --> 00:00:09.080
Countless hopefuls have auditioned for the
part of Peter Parker in his various incarnations

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:12.940
over the years, including some of the biggest
and most unexpected names in Hollywood.

00:00:12.940 --> 00:00:16.379
We'll never know what might have happened
if one of these guys had been cast as Spidey,

00:00:16.379 --> 00:00:17.470
but it's fun to wonder.

00:00:17.470 --> 00:00:21.029
Here's a look at some actors who were almost
cast as Spider-Man.

00:00:21.029 --> 00:00:22.279
Heath Ledger

00:00:22.279 --> 00:00:25.919
Heath Ledger will forever be remembered for
his game-changing performance as the Joker

00:00:25.919 --> 00:00:28.029
in Christopher Nolan's The Dark Knight.

00:00:28.029 --> 00:00:31.240
But the late actor could have wound up on
the good guys' side if Sony had gotten its

00:00:31.240 --> 00:00:32.240
way.

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.750
In the wake of his death, Ledger's former
agent Steve Alexander revealed that his client

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:39.770
was the studio's number one choice when it
came to casting the lead in Sam Raimi's Spider-Man.

00:00:39.770 --> 00:00:43.800
The actor was ultimately put off by how little
he knew about the character, telling his agent

00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:47.010
he would have been "taking somebody else's
dream away" if he took the part without fully

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.230
appreciating Spider-Man's history.

00:00:49.230 --> 00:00:50.780
Jake Gyllenhal

00:00:50.780 --> 00:00:54.980
Of course, the part of Raimi's Peter Parker
ultimately went to Tobey Maguire, and the

00:00:54.980 --> 00:00:56.210
rest is Spidey history.

00:00:56.210 --> 00:01:00.739
Maguire was propelled to A-list stardom after
the first film stormed to the top of the domestic

00:01:00.739 --> 00:01:05.220
box office in 2003, but he almost never made
it to the second movie after sustaining a

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.549
back injury while filming Seabiscuit in 2003.

00:01:08.549 --> 00:01:12.320
Jake Gyllenhaal was drafted to take his place,
though he was dropped when Maguire made a

00:01:12.320 --> 00:01:14.970
miraculous recovery just in time for shooting.

00:01:14.970 --> 00:01:18.979
It isn't the only time Gyllenhaal has narrowly
missed out on playing an iconic superhero:

00:01:18.979 --> 00:01:23.369
he was also close to playing Christopher Nolan's
Batman, only to be beaten to the role by Christian

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:24.369
Bale.

00:01:24.369 --> 00:01:26.290
"I'm Batman!"

00:01:26.290 --> 00:01:27.450
James Franco

00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:31.039
It's not uncommon for an actor to come away
from an audition having failed to get the

00:01:31.039 --> 00:01:35.310
part that they went for, only to be contacted
and offered another role in the picture.

00:01:35.310 --> 00:01:39.070
This was the case with James Franco, who read
for the lead role in Sam Raimi's Spider-Man

00:01:39.070 --> 00:01:42.420
but was ultimately beaten to the prize by
Tobey Maguire.

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:48.000
"Oh, look at little Goblin Jr. Gonna cry?"

00:01:51.630 --> 00:01:54.960
Franco confessed that he was disappointed
when the director called him to let him know

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:58.370
the part had gone to Maguire, though he softened
the blow when he instead offered the Freaks

00:01:58.370 --> 00:02:02.880
and Geeks star the part of Harry Osborn, son
of the Green Goblin and Peter Parker's best

00:02:02.880 --> 00:02:03.880
friend.

00:02:03.880 --> 00:02:07.030
Franco agreed, and the casting call for Harry
was immediately withdrawn.

00:02:07.030 --> 00:02:08.470
Daniel Radcliffe

00:02:08.470 --> 00:02:12.350
When Tom Holland was announced as the newest
Spider-Man, Harry Potter star Daniel Radcliffe

00:02:12.350 --> 00:02:15.270
was among those who offered public congratulations
to Holland.

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:17.520
But his compliments may have come through
gritted teeth.

00:02:17.520 --> 00:02:21.480
Radcliffe went on to reveal that he wanted
the part himself while also conceding that

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:24.880
it was now out of reach: "I would have made
a good Spider-Man, but the boat has sailed

00:02:24.880 --> 00:02:25.880
on that one.

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.670
I'm not sure if I'd sign up for something
that was another seven or eight films or ten

00:02:28.670 --> 00:02:30.500
years, but a shorter franchise, yeah."

00:02:30.500 --> 00:02:34.800
He did get to at least out his dream by cosplaying
as Spider-Man for Comic-Con, though, so that's

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:35.800
nice.

00:02:35.800 --> 00:02:39.420
"You don't go to Comic-Con without going down
on the floor and seeing it all, and so the

00:02:39.420 --> 00:02:42.110
way I came up with doing that was Spider-Man."

00:02:42.110 --> 00:02:43.620
Leonardo DiCaprio

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:48.069
Just because we didn't get a big-budget Spider-Man
until 2002, that doesn't mean there weren't

00:02:48.069 --> 00:02:49.069
earlier attempts.

00:02:49.069 --> 00:02:53.290
One of the big what-ifs in superhero movies
is the James Cameron version of Spider-Man,

00:02:53.290 --> 00:02:56.650
which the acclaimed director abandoned in
favor of Titanic.

00:02:56.650 --> 00:03:00.820
Among the stars Cameron considered for the
lead role was none other than Leonardo DiCaprio,

00:03:00.820 --> 00:03:04.000
who read the screenplay and met with Cameron
to discuss the role.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.950
DiCaprio never did get to don those tightie-Spideys,
but Cameron gave him a pretty good consolation

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:10.900
prize: the lead role in Titanic.

00:03:10.900 --> 00:03:15.220
"I'm the king of the world!

00:03:15.220 --> 00:03:16.220
Woo!"

00:03:16.220 --> 00:03:19.120
And that's not even the earliest try at a
cinematic Spidey...

00:03:19.120 --> 00:03:20.690
Tom Cruise

00:03:20.690 --> 00:03:24.430
After several failed attempts to negotiate
a deal to get Spider-Man swinging onto the

00:03:24.430 --> 00:03:28.450
big screen in the late '70s and early '80s,
Marvel finally signed on the dotted line with

00:03:28.450 --> 00:03:32.570
Cannon Films, a production company known for
churning out low-budget action films.

00:03:32.570 --> 00:03:36.810
Texas Chainsaw Massacre director Tobe Hooper
was signed up to direct a script that actually

00:03:36.810 --> 00:03:40.330
saw Peter Parker mutate into an anthropomorphic
tarantula.

00:03:40.330 --> 00:03:44.410
Tom Cruise was offered the part, opposite
Bob Hoskins as iconic Spidey villain Doctor

00:03:44.410 --> 00:03:49.020
Octopus...but, unsurprisingly, Cruise decided
to pass on the bizarre project.

00:03:49.020 --> 00:03:51.330
But wait — it gets even weirder!

00:03:51.330 --> 00:03:53.530
Michael Jackson

00:03:53.530 --> 00:03:57.710
In 2009, Marvel's legendary Stan Lee told
bewildered audiences at San Diego Comic-Con

00:03:57.710 --> 00:04:01.690
that Michael Jackson had once invited him
to his Neverland ranch to ask about personally

00:04:01.690 --> 00:04:05.530
producing a Spider-Man movie, with the late
King of Pop intending to take on the starring

00:04:05.530 --> 00:04:06.530
role himself.

00:04:06.530 --> 00:04:10.130
When Marvel said no, Jackson came up with
a new plan: buy Marvel out so he could play

00:04:10.130 --> 00:04:11.130
the role!

00:04:11.130 --> 00:04:15.490
"When I told him I didn't think the company
would sell it, he then wanted to buy Marvel.

00:04:15.490 --> 00:04:17.150
'So then I'll buy Marvel!'

00:04:17.150 --> 00:04:19.139
I don't know what happened.

00:04:19.139 --> 00:04:23.240
Obviously he didn't buy Marvel, but he wanted
to very much."

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.240
Yikes.

00:04:24.240 --> 00:04:27.400
My hee-hee sense is tingling!

